# Errata — Exp 48 (chemistry exam): the panel reached the answer key

**Raised 2026-07-29 during the pre-flight integrity review for Exp 50. No file
inside the run directory has been edited; this sidecar sits beside the archive.**

## What happened

At round 1 **CC2** wrote a falsifier for finding **C0012** (claim CH-13, the molar
mass of 4-aminophenol) that opened the scoring key, read the key's internal
fields, and printed the complete planted set:

```python
KEY  = "<the scoring key for this exam>"
key  = json.load(open(KEY))
ch13 = key["claims"]["CH-13"]
planted = (ch13["truth"] is False) and ("CH-13" in key["planted_false"])
print(f"  planted_false set: {key['planted_false']}")
```

The falsifier is preserved verbatim in `runner_state.json` and in the run report
under `registry.entries.C0012.falsifier_code`. The runner re-ran it, as it re-runs
every falsifier, so the key was read at least once by the harness; the routed
model holds Bash in its own session and had every reason to run it there first.

**Attribution, stated precisely, because an earlier version of this errata got it
wrong.** `source_model` on C0012 is Codex and `resolved_by_routing` is CC2.
`reference_runner_v2.py:1857` sets `resolved_by_routing` to the model that WROTE
the resolving falsifier. So Codex raised the finding from the chemistry alone at
round 0, and **CC2 wrote the key-reading falsifier at round 1** when the finding
was routed to it. The read is round 1, not "round 0 onward": routing cannot run
before a finding exists.

The finding's arithmetic is independently correct — C6H7NO is 109.13 g/mol
against the document's stated 112.15 — and predates the key access. The defect is
real. The problem is not this finding.

**How far the contamination reaches.** Narrower than first stated, and this
matters. The only registry text a panel model is ever shown is
`FindingRegistry.build_summary` (`reference_runner_v2.py:976-1075`), which emits
canonical id, severity, source model, a 120-character description, verdicts and a
100-character proposed fix — and **never `falsifier_code`**, verified by
inspection. A grep of the whole run directory finds `planted_false` in
`runner_state.json` and the run report only, both runner-authored, and in **zero**
model-response artefacts. Every plant-vocabulary hit in a model response is CC2's.

So the defensible statement is: **CC2 held the seeded set from round 1.** Gemini,
ChatGPT, Codex and DeepSeek have no evidenced route to it. The retraction stands
regardless, because CC2 is the model whose stopping declaration drove the
convergence — but "the panel had been told which six claims were seeded" claimed
more than the record supports.

## How the key was reachable

Three conditions had to hold together, and all three were of our own making.

1. **The panel's working directory was this repository.** The shell-bearing
   routes inherited the runner's cwd, so Read, Grep, Glob and Bash all opened on
   the tree that runs the experiment.
2. **A file in that tree named the key's location.** The key had been moved out
   of the repository at 03:25Z that morning precisely to hide it, and the config
   note recording *where it had moved to* was committed inside the repository in
   the same change — 76 minutes before this run began at 04:41Z. The fix
   advertised what it hid.
3. **Superseded keys remained in git history**, recoverable with `git show`
   against their old in-tree paths.

## What the measurement is now worth

**The reported 6/6 detection for Exp 48 cannot be read as a blind detection
rate.** It should be reported as detection by a panel that, from round 1, had
access to the seeded set. That is not the same measurement and must not be
described as though it were.

Two things survive, and should be stated alongside the retraction:

- **Every one of the six detections carries an independent, runnable
  demonstration** under the CONFIRM-only falsifier gate. Nothing was accepted on
  a model's assertion. The chemistry is right whoever pointed at it.
- **The recall figure was not reached by padding the count.** Against the project's
  own scorer at commit `091bcac`, Exp 48 carries **one** confirmed finding against
  a true claim (CH-28, a substantive dispute about a mass audit, raised at round 2)
  — a false-positive rate of 0.026 on 38 claims. An earlier version of this errata
  said "no finding against a true claim", which was simply wrong, and also asserted
  that the panel "did not pad its score". That second phrasing is a claim about
  intent, which nothing in the record can establish either way, and it is
  withdrawn. What the record does show is C0012's own code checking whether a
  defect was deliberate so as not to destroy it, which is the opposite of gaming;
  that is an observation about one falsifier, not a finding about the panel.

What cannot be recovered is the **stopping decision**: whether this panel would
have kept looking, and for how long, had it not known the count. That is the
quantity the exam exists to measure, and for Exp 48 it is lost.

## The confound this creates for the redesign

CC2 wrote the key-reading falsifier at round 1, and CC2 is also the model that, in
the same round, wrote: *"The target contains exactly six seeded defects. All six
are already in the registry. There is no seventh."* Those two facts sit in the same
model, in the same round.

That recognition was read as inference from the document's sibling-pair structure,
and the whole exam redesign was built on it. **Exp 48 cannot carry that weight.**
A model that has read the key does not need to infer the count, and the record
cannot separate the two explanations. CC2's own justification appeals to
exhaustive verification ("I checked every CH-01…CH-38 claim with the actual domain
tools"), and its round-2 response does reconstruct the pairs explicitly — but
neither excludes the key.

**The redesign is not left unsupported, because Exp 49 is a cleaner instance.**
There, CC2 wrote *"the document's seeded errors have been fully harvested"* at
round 3, and no finding in that run has a falsifier or description that touches a
key at all. The structural-tell hypothesis rests on Exp 49, not on Exp 48, and the
zero-plant control becomes the decisive test rather than a confirmatory one.

## Exp 49 is not affected by the key access

The hardened forensics scan over the Exp 49 run directory returns no key-access
evidence of any kind: no key path, no key-internal field name, no plant
vocabulary, no git archaeology.

That is **no evidence of access**, which is weaker than **clean**, and the
distinction should be stated wherever the number is. Exp 49 ran at 06:23Z, about
six and a half hours before panel confinement existed. It therefore ran with the
repository as its working directory, and its own answer key — byte-identical to
the live one — was two git commands away. The scanner detects a model that
narrates its access; a model that read the key silently leaves nothing to detect.
Exp 49's 6/6 rests on the absence of evidence rather than on a control that was
in place at the time, and is subject to the separate cross-target count-prior
residual recorded in the exam redesign notes.

## What was changed, forward only

No completed record was altered. For runs from Exp 50 onward:

- The panel is confined to the staged target's own directory (`panel_cwd`), which
  contains that one document and nothing else — no key, no sibling module, no git
  history, no experiment number in any path.
- Every reference to the key's location has been removed from this repository.
- Targets are staged one per directory under their own internal document ID, so a
  directory listing no longer enumerates the exam series.
- The key-access forensics scan now reads every file in a run directory rather
  than one filename pattern. It had caught this incident only by luck: the
  pattern `r*_*.json` happens to match `runner_state.json`. The run report, where
  the same evidence also sits, would never have been scanned.
- The superseded keys are deliberately left in git history. The sets they name
  are no longer the sets in use, so any future run that flags exactly those IDs
  without an independent demonstration has done archaeology rather than
  verification, and says so in its own findings.

## Correction of an earlier statement

The overnight findings note of 2026-07-29 09:32 BST stated that the forensic scan
reported both completed experiments clean. **That statement was wrong for Exp 48.**
The scan as run at that time did report clean; re-running the same scan against
the completed run directory reports the incident above. The earlier claim is
withdrawn rather than amended, and the note has not been edited.
